course: language
lesson: 52
title: Sarcasm, hyperbole and understatement side by side
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Three kinds of talk bend the truth in three different ways. Hyperbole makes things BIGGER («we'll be here till we're ninety»), understatement makes them SMALLER («bit of a wait, eh»), and sarcasm says the OPPOSITE («oh brilliant, I LOVE standing in the rain»).
explain: Picture one wet bus stop with the bus twenty minutes late: Ruby says «This bus is NEVER coming», her brother Jack says «Oh brilliant, best day ever», and their gran says «Bit of a wait, eh». Three people, three kinds of talk, one fed-up feeling.
explain: To tell them apart, put the words next to the facts and ask: is it the opposite of the truth (sarcasm), bigger than the truth (hyperbole) or smaller than the truth (understatement)?
explain: The same words can be real or not, depending on the scene: «Nice one, Kai!» after a goal is praise, and after a dropped tray it is sarcasm. And some true facts sound unbelievable — a science book is not a playground boast.
explain: In a text there is no voice to help, so if you can't tell, just ask.
fact: Hyperbole makes something bigger than it is: «This bus is NEVER coming» means «I'm fed up with waiting»; the bus will come.
fact: Sarcasm says the opposite of what is meant, usually to tease or complain: «Oh brilliant, I LOVE standing in the rain» means «I hate this».
fact: Understatement makes something smaller than it is: Gran's «Bit of a wait, eh» means a long, annoying wait.
fact: «Slightly well done» about black, smoking toast is understatement: it is burnt.
fact: «Mmm, delicious charcoal, chef's kiss» is sarcasm: the words say «delicious», the toast says «burnt».
fact: «You burnt the WHOLE HOUSE down!» about one piece of toast is hyperbole; if there were real flames or smoke filling the room, that would be an emergency, so look at what is actually happening.
fact: People can mix the kinds: «Oh yeah, I've ONLY told you a million times» uses sarcastic «only» (it's a lot) and hyperbolic «a million».
fact: The situation decides the meaning: the same words, «Nice one, Kai!», are praise after a goal and sarcasm after he drops his tray.
fact: In a text message there is no voice or face, so «great, that took forever» could be real relief or sarcasm; it is fine to ask «u annoyed or just joking?», and tone tags like «/j» (joking) can help.
fact: Some true facts sound like hyperbole: scientists reported in 2014 that a blue whale's heart weighed about 180 kilograms, as much as two or three grown men.
fact: «Hmm, not bad. Not bad at all, kiddo» about 19 out of 20 is understatement (litotes): Dad is proud.
exercise: At the rainy bus stop Ruby says «This bus is NEVER coming, we'll be here till we're ninety». What kind of talk is it? | Hyperbole | Sarcasm | Understatement | A plain fact
exercise: Jack says «Oh brilliant, I LOVE standing in the rain, best day ever». What does he mean? | He hates standing in the rain | He really loves the rain today | He thinks it is a fairly good day | He is happy the bus is so late
exercise: Gran says «Bit of a wait, eh». What kind of talk is it? | Understatement | Hyperbole | Sarcasm | A plain true fact
exercise: Which kind of talk says the OPPOSITE of what is meant? | Sarcasm | Hyperbole | Understatement | Litotes
exercise: Dad's toast comes out black and smoking: «Hmm. Slightly well done». What does he mean? | It's burnt | It's just a bit dark and fine to eat | It's cooked perfectly | It's not cooked enough
exercise: Big sister Mia says «Mmm, delicious charcoal, chef's kiss». What kind of talk is it? | Sarcasm, because she means the toast is bad | Hyperbole, because she makes the toast bigger | Understatement, because she makes it smaller | A plain fact, because charcoal is delicious
exercise: Little brother Theo shouts «You burnt the WHOLE HOUSE down!». What is really burnt? | One piece of toast | The whole house | The kitchen | The kitchen curtains
exercise: When would «there's smoke everywhere» NOT be hyperbole? | When there are real flames or smoke filling the room | When a little brother says it about toast | When someone says it in a very loud and frightened voice | When it is said in the morning
exercise: Mum, in a flat voice: «Oh yeah, I've ONLY told you a million times». Which words are sarcasm and which are hyperbole? | «Only» is sarcasm and «a million» is hyperbole | «Only» is hyperbole and «a million» is sarcasm | Both words are understatement | Neither, it is all a plain fact
exercise: «Nice one, Kai!» after a goal, then «Nice one, Kai!» after he drops his tray. What changes the meaning? | The situation | The words that are said | The spelling | Kai's name
exercise: Lily texts «great, that took forever» after waiting for a reply. Why is it hard to tell what she means? | In a text there's no voice or face to help | Texts always mean the opposite of the words | Lily always tells lies in her messages | The word «great» has no meaning in texts
exercise: What can you send if you are not sure how a text was meant? | «u annoyed or just joking?» | «you're so rude» | Nothing ever again | A thumbs down
exercise: What does the tone tag «/j» mean at the end of a message? | Joking | Jealous | Just now | Jump in
exercise: A nature book says a blue whale's heart can weigh about 180 kilograms. What kind of sentence is it? | A real fact from scientists | Hyperbole, like a boast | Sarcasm about whales | Understatement about hearts
exercise: Dad looks at Theo's maths test, 19 out of 20: «Hmm, not bad. Not bad at all, kiddo». How does he feel? | Proud | Disappointed | Angry | Bored
exercise: Which question helps you tell the three kinds apart? | Is it the opposite, bigger or smaller than the truth? | Is the person speaking loudly or quietly? | Is it said in Britain or in America? | Is the sentence long or short?
test: «Jack said he loves the rain so I'll ask him to go to the park in it lol» What has gone wrong? | He was being sarcastic: he meant he hates it | Nothing, he really does love the rain | He was using hyperbole about the bus | He was using understatement about the rain and the bus
test: Gran says «bit of a wait» after twenty minutes in the rain. «Gran doesn't mind waiting,» says Ruby's friend. What is right? | Gran minds; she's playing it down | Gran loves waiting at rainy bus stops | Gran has only been there one minute | Gran is being very sarcastic about it
test: Theo yells «the WHOLE HOUSE is burnt!» Should you call the fire brigade? | Not for one burnt piece of toast, but yes if there were real flames | Yes, every single time anyone shouts about something burning in the house | Never, because it's always hyperbole | Only if Dad says «slightly well done»
test: ★ Mia's «delicious charcoal» and Dad's «slightly well done» are about the same toast. What is different? | Mia says the opposite; Dad makes it smaller | Mia makes it bigger; Dad says the opposite | Both of them are using hyperbole | Both of them really like the toast
test: ★ «The bus is NEVER coming» (Ruby), «Oh brilliant» (Jack), «bit of a wait» (Gran). Which one makes things smaller? | Gran's | Ruby's | Jack's | None
test: ★★ Your friend texts «great, that took forever». You're not sure if she's cross. What is the best thing to do? | Ask her kindly whether she's annoyed or joking | Get upset and stop talking to her for a whole week | Decide she is cross and say sorry ten times | Decide she is joking and ignore it
test: ★★ «Amir's book says a whale's heart weighs as much as two or three men, that's hyperbole innit» What is right? | No, it's a real measured fact in a science book | Yes, books exaggerate to sell, like adverts | Yes, no heart can weigh more than a kilo | No, it's understatement: the real heart is much bigger
test: ★★ Theo's dad says «not bad at all, kiddo» about 19 out of 20, and Theo sulks: «he wanted 20». What has Theo missed? | It was understatement, and Dad is really pleased | Dad is cross that he missed one mark | Dad thinks 19 is a bad score for a test | Dad was being sarcastic about the test
test: ★★★ Mum says flatly «Oh great, you've ONLY left your towel there a million times». Your little brother says «she said great, so she's happy». How many kinds of talk did Mum use, and is she happy? | Two, sarcasm and hyperbole; she's cross | One, a plain fact; she's happy | Two, understatement and litotes; she's happy | Three, all three kinds; she doesn't care
test: ★★★ At the bus stop the real wait is 20 minutes. Ruby says «forever», Gran says «a few minutes», the timetable app says «20 min». Who used hyperbole, who used understatement, and who gave the fact? | Ruby, Gran, the app | Gran, Ruby, the app | The app, Ruby, Gran | Ruby, the app, Gran
exam: Identify the figure employed in the utterance «Oh brilliant, I LOVE standing in the rain», made by a pupil detained at a bus stop in heavy rain. | Sarcasm, the expressed meaning being the opposite of that intended | Hyperbole, the expressed meaning being an enlargement of the truth | Understatement, the expressed meaning being a reduction of the truth | A literal declaration of preference
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the description «slightly well done», applied to toast which is black and emits smoke? | Understatement | Hyperbole | Sarcasm | A literal description
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning identical words, «Nice one», uttered first after a goal and subsequently after a dropped tray. | The meaning is determined by the circumstances, the second instance being sarcastic | The meaning is identical on both occasions, as the words are identical | Both utterances constitute sincere praise | Both utterances constitute hyperbole
exam: It is stated in a work of natural history that the heart of a blue whale may weigh approximately one hundred and eighty kilograms. Ascertain the correct classification of this statement. | A reported measurement of fact | An instance of hyperbole | An instance of sarcasm | An instance of understatement
